<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.9.5: Interpret Graphs to find Approximate Values</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01400</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.9.5: Interpret Graphs to find Approximate Values</md:title>
    <md:uuid>0aaf3ff7-18fa-4a1a-97e0-519784ec92f3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>In case students are unfamiliar with antibiotics, explain that they kill bacteria and keep bacteria from reproducing.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>The graph shows the bacteria population on a petri dish as a function \(f\)<emphasis> </emphasis>of the days \(d\) since an antibiotic is introduced.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of function."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8ac459d6b679859876d01a57b6c7aceefa5ad64d"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the approximate value of \(f(4.5)\)? 
    
    <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>The exact value is 622,431, but reasonable approximations should be greater than 600,000 and less than 650,000. 
    
  </item>
  <item> Approximately what is \(d\) when \(f(d)=400,000\)? 
    
    <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>The value to one decimal place is 8.7, but reasonable approximations should be greater than 8.5 and less than 9. 
    
  </item>
  <item> Explain what you would do, using your usual graphing technology, to be able to see \(f(15)\) on the graph. 
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Answers vary based on what graphing technology is used.
  </item>
</list>
<title> Response to Student Thinking</title>
<para>Points to emphasize:</para>
<para>Students will have more opportunities to interpret graphs, so use the results from this cool-down to choose points to emphasize in subsequent lessons and highlight and reinforce strategies for identifying points on graphs.</para></section>
</content>
